+<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Are there homes with a pool under $300,000 in Myrtle Beach?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Very few. On November 8, 2026, 5 of the 575 houses for sale in Horry County at $300,000 or less had their own pool. 168 houses at that price sat in a neighborhood with a shared pool."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does a pool add value to a home in Myrtle Beach?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Houses with a pool ask more at every size, but they also sit nearer the beach and more often on the water. The gap in asking prices is not the value of the pool itself."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do you need a fence around a pool in South Carolina?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"South Carolina has no statewide pool code; each county or city may adopt one. Horry County requires a barrier at least 48 inches tall around a pool outside city limits."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do home inspectors check pools in South Carolina?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Not by default. The standard South Carolina home inspectors follow does not require them to inspect a pool, a hot tub or a fence. The buyer and the inspector can agree to add it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I rent out a house with a pool in Myrtle Beach?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A pool at a single-family home, including a short-term rental, is a residential pool, and the state pool office does not regulate it. Renting the pool itself out to the public, for example through a pool-sharing app, needs a state public-pool permit."}}]}</script>
