"list" class="c3-glance" style="--n:3"><li><span class="i"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M9 15V5a2 2 0 0 0-2-2M15 15V5a2 2 0 0 0-2-2M9 7h6M9 11h6M2 18c1.7 1.2 3.3 1.2 5 0s3.3-1.2 5 0 3.3 1.2 5 0 3.3-1.2 5 0M2 21.5c1.7 1.2 3.3 1.2 5 0s3.3-1.2 5 0 3.3 1.2 5 0 3.3-1.2 5 0"/></svg></span><span><strong>257 of about 2,950</strong><span class="t">houses for sale have their own pool</span></span></li><li><span class="i"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M15 9.4c-.4-1-1.5-1.8-3-1.8-1.8 0-3 1-3 2.2 0 3 6 1.6 6 4.4 0 1.3-1.3 2.2-3 2.2-1.6 0-2.7-.7-3.1-1.8M12 5.8v1.8M12 16.4v1.8"/><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9"/></svg></span><span><strong>About $955,000</strong><span class="t">middle asking price of a house with a pool</span></span></li><li><span class="i"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M3 11.5 12 3.5l9 8M5.5 10v10.5h13V10M10 20.5V15h4v5.5"/></svg></span><span><strong>About 1,260</strong><span class="t">houses sit in a neighborhood with a shared pool</span></span></li></ul></div><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem;margin-bottom:1rem">By <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Devin Day</strong>, Operations Officer &middot; Reviewed by <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Tim Nash</strong>, Broker-in-Charge &middot; <span style="white-space:nowrap">Updated November 8, 2026</span></p><p class="detail-sub">Where the pool homes are, what they ask, and what Horry County requires around a pool.</p></div></div><section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The short answer</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">About 1 in 11 houses for sale in Horry County has its own pool. The middle one asks about $955,000, against about $400,000 for a house without one.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Under $500,000, about 1 house in 50 has a pool. Most buyers at that price get a pool another way. About 1,260 houses for sale sit in neighborhoods with a shared pool, with a middle asking price of about $435,000. The homeowners association (HOA) fee there is about $105 a month.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--navy);padding:3rem 0"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:center;max-width:720px;margin:0 auto"><p style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--ivory);margin-bottom:1rem">Every house with a pool for sale now, on one map.</p><a class="btn btn-brass btn-lg" href="/homes/map/?q=house%20with%20a%20pool">See the pool homes</a></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory);padding-top:2.5rem"><div class="wrap"><nav aria-label="On this page" style="margin-top:0;max-width:720px"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.3rem;font-weight:600">On this page</p><ul style="list-style:none;margin:0;padding:0"><li><a href="#where" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Where they are</a></li><li><a href="#cost" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">What they cost</a></li><li><a href="#own-or-shared" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Own pool or shared</a></li><li><a href="#fence" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">The fence rule</a></li><li><a href="#inspection" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">The inspection</a></li><li><a href="#costs" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Insurance and tax</a></li><li><a href="#pool-homes-faq" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Pool homes FAQ</a></li></ul></nav></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="where"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Where they are">Where are the homes for sale with a pool in Myrtle Beach?</h2><style>.c3ph-f{position:relative;margin:0;border-radius:6px;overflow:hidden;background:var(--ivory-2)}.c3ph-f img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;object-fit:cover}.c3ph-cr{position:absolute;right:0;bottom:0;max-width:100%;padding:1.6rem .6rem .35rem 2.6rem;font:400 .625rem/1.3 var(--sans);letter-spacing:.01em;color:#fff;text-align:right;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 100%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0));white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;pointer-events:none}.c3ph-cr a{color:#fff;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(255,255,255,.5);text-underline-offset:2px;pointer-events:auto;padding:10px 0 6px}.c3ph-hero{margin:1.6rem 0 2rem}.c3ph-hero .c3ph-f{border-radius:8px}.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:4/3}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:16/9}}.c3ph-hero figcaption{color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.5;margin-top:.55rem}.c3ph-gal{display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:1.5rem;margin:1.6rem 0;padding:0;list-style:none}.c3ph-gal>figure{margin:0;min-width:0}.c3ph-gal img{aspect-ratio:var(--c3ph-r,4/3)}.c3ph-gal figcaption{color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.45;margin-top:.5rem}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-gal{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(var(--c3ph-n,3),minmax(0,1fr));gap:1rem;overflow:visible;padding:0}.c3ph-gal>figure{min-width:0}}@media (max-width:699px){.c3ph-gal.c3ph-row{display:flex;gap:.75rem;padding:0 var(--c3ph-bleed,1.4rem) .4rem;margin:1.6rem calc(-1*var(--c3ph-bleed,1.4rem)) 0;overflow-x:auto;scroll-snap-type:x mandatory;scroll-padding:0 var(--c3ph-bleed,1.4rem);-webkit-overflow-scrolling:touch;scrollbar-width:none}.c3ph-row::-webkit-scrollbar{display:none}.c3ph-row>figure{flex:0 0 82%;scroll-snap-align:start}}.c3ph-swipe{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.75rem;margin:.5rem 0 1.6rem;color:var(--navy);font:500 .95rem/1.3 var(--sans)}.c3ph-dots{display:inline-flex;gap:.45rem}.c3ph-dots i{display:block;width:10px;height:10px;border-radius:50%;border:1.5px solid var(--navy);box-sizing:border-box}.c3ph-dots i:first-child{background:var(--navy)}@supports (animation-timeline:view()){.c3ph-rowbox{timeline-scope:--c3ph-p1,--c3ph-p2,--c3ph-p3}.c3ph-row>figure:nth-child(1){view-timeline:--c3ph-p1 inline}.c3ph-row>figure:nth-child(2){view-timeline:--c3ph-p2 inline}.c3ph-row>figure:nth-child(3){view-timeline:--c3ph-p3 inline}.c3ph-dots i{animation:c3ph-dot linear both;animation-range:cover}.c3ph-dots i:nth-child(1){animation-timeline:--c3ph-p1}.c3ph-dots i:nth-child(2){animation-timeline:--c3ph-p2}.c3ph-dots i:nth-child(3){animation-timeline:--c3ph-p3}@keyframes c3ph-dot{0%,36%{background:transparent}42%,60%{background:var(--navy)}66%,100%{background:transparent}}}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-swipe{display:none}}.c3ph-cards{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr));gap:.75rem;margin:1.4rem 0 1.8rem;padding:0;list-style:none}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-cards{grid-template-columns:repeat(3,minmax(0,1fr));gap:1rem}}@media (min-width:1000px){.c3ph-cards.c3ph-4{grid-template-columns:repeat(4,minmax(0,1fr))}}.c3ph-card{margin:0;background:var(--ivory);border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:6px;overflow:hidden;display:flex;flex-direction:column}.c3ph-card .c3ph-f{border-radius:0}.c3ph-card img,.c3ph-card svg{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;aspect-ratio:var(--c3ph-r,4/3);object-fit:cover}.c3ph-card .c3ph-cr{font-size:.56rem;padding:1.2rem .45rem .25rem 1.6rem}.c3ph-card strong{display:block;color:var(--navy);font-size:.98rem;font-weight:500;line-height:1.3;padding:.7rem .8rem 0}.c3ph-card span.c3ph-t{display:block;color:var(--muted);font-size:.86rem;line-height:1.45;padding:.2rem .8rem .85rem}.c3ph-credits{margin:2rem 0 1rem;padding:1rem 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted);font-size:.8rem;line-height:1.55;max-width:760px}.c3ph-credits .c3ph-h{font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;font-weight:500;color:var(--navy);margin:0 0 .4rem}.c3ph-credits [role=listitem]{margin:0 0 .3rem}.c3ph-credits a{color:inherit;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(28,32,40,.3);text-underline-offset:2px}</style><style>section[style*="background:var(--ivory)"] .c3ph-card{background:var(--ivory-2)}.c3ph-cards{max-width:920px}.c3ph-card strong{font-family:var(--sans)}.c3ph-card span.c3ph-t{font-size:1rem!important}.c3ph-card strong{font-size:1rem!important}ul[role=list] li span[style*="font-size:.9rem"]{font-size:1rem!important}section#c3-photo{padding-top:0}section:has(+section#c3-photo){background:var(--ivory-2)!important;padding-bottom:2rem}.c3ph-hero{margin:0}.c3-aerial .c3ph-hero{max-width:900px;margin:1.4rem 0 1.6rem}.c3-aerial .c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:auto!important;height:auto}.c3-aerial-close .c3ph-hero{max-width:640px}.c3-aerial-ctop .c3ph-cr{top:0;bottom:auto;padding:.35rem .6rem 1.6rem 2.6rem;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 0%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0))}@media (max-width:699px){.c3ph-cards>:last-child:nth-child(odd){grid-column:1/-1}}.c3-maps{display:grid;gap:0 1.5rem;max-width:1136px}@media (min-width:900px){.c3-maps{grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;align-items:start}}.c3-maps figure{max-width:none!important;margin:1.4rem 0 .4rem!important}.c3-maps svg,.c3-maps img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto}.c3-map-links{row-gap:0!important}.c3-map-links a{padding:.55rem 0}ul[role=list][style*="minmax(13rem,1fr)"]{grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(min(13rem,100%),1fr))!important}</style><style>.c3-aerial .c3ph-hero,.c3-aerial-close .c3ph-hero{max-width:720px}</style><div class="pl-side"><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Most are in Myrtle Beach and North Myrtle Beach, and they sit near the ocean. About 1 in 3 is east of US 17, the highway that runs along the coast, against about 1 in 6 of all houses for sale.</p><div class="c3-aerial"><figure class="c3ph-hero" style=""><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-11-n/buyers-pool-homes/surfside-beach-houses-with-pools-2011-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-11-n/buyers-pool-homes/surfside-beach-houses-with-pools-2011-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-11-n/buyers-pool-homes/surfside-beach-houses-with-pools-2011-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1136px" width="1200" height="675" alt="Four beach houses in a row, each with a small fenced pool between the house and the dunes, with wooden walkways over the dunes to the beach" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://www.usa.gov/publicdomain/label/1.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="USGS photo, 2011. License: Public domain" title="License: Public domain">USGS photo, 2011</a></span></div><figcaption>Four oceanfront houses in Surfside Beach, each with its own pool behind the dunes, seen from a plane in 2011.</figcaption></figure></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Surfside Beach and the Garden City area have the highest share. In each, 1 house in 4 or more for sale has its own pool. Our <a href="/buyers/homes-near-the-beach/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">homes near the beach page</a> shows how far each area is from the sand.</p></div><table class="pl-towns"><caption>Houses for sale with their own pool, by town</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Town</th><th scope="col">With a pool</th><th scope="col">Middle ask, with a pool</th><th scope="col">Middle ask, without</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/homes/map/?type=house&amp;city=Myrtle%20Beach&amp;has=pool,county_horry">Myrtle Beach</a></th><td data-l="With a pool">113 of 1,049</td><td data-l="Middle ask, with a pool">$1,120,000</td><td data-l="Middle ask, without">$460,000</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/homes/map/?type=house&amp;city=North%20Myrtle%20Beach&amp;has=pool,county_horry">North Myrtle Beach</a></th><td data-l="With a pool">47 of 257</td><td data-l="Middle ask, with a pool">$1,000,000</td><td data-l="Middle ask, without">$650,000</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/homes/map/?type=house&amp;has=pool,area_carolina_forest,county_horry">Carolina Forest area</a></th><td data-l="With a pool">33 of 300</td><td data-l="Middle ask, with a pool">$985,000</td><td data-l="Middle ask, without">$465,000</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/homes/map/?type=house&amp;city=Conway&amp;has=pool,county_horry">Conway</a></th><td data-l="With a pool">23 of 643</td><td data-l="Middle ask, with a pool">$515,000</td><td data-l="Middle ask, without">$325,000</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/homes/map/?type=house&amp;city=Surfside%20Beach&amp;has=pool,county_horry">Surfside Beach</a></th><td data-l="With a pool">21 of 84</td><td data-l="Middle ask, with a pool">$1,250,000</td><td data-l="Middle ask, without">$500,000</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/homes/map/?type=house&amp;city=Murrells%20Inlet&amp;has=pool,county_horry">Murrells Inlet, Horry County side</a></th><td data-l="With a pool">20 of 134</td><td data-l="Middle ask, with a pool">$1,000,000</td><td data-l="Middle ask, without">$530,000</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/homes/map/?type=house&amp;city=Longs&amp;has=pool,county_horry">Longs</a></th><td data-l="With a pool">14 of 325</td><td data-l="Middle ask, with a pool">$435,000</td><td data-l="Middle ask, without">$320,000</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/homes/map/?type=house&amp;has=pool,area_garden_city,county_horry">Garden City area</a></th><td data-l="With a pool">11 of 36</td><td data-l="Middle ask, with a pool">$1,000,000</td><td data-l="Middle ask, without">$730,000</td></tr><tr class="all"><th scope="row">All of Horry County</th><td data-l="With a pool">257 of 2,950</td><td data-l="Middle ask, with a pool">$955,000</td><td data-l="Middle ask, without">$400,000</td></tr></tbody></table><p class="pl-note">Our count of the houses for sale on our site on November 8, 2026, from CCMLS, the local listing service. A town is the mailing address, not city limits. Carolina Forest and Garden City are areas the listing service draws, and they overlap the towns, so the rows do not add up. Above-ground pools are not counted. Each town name opens its pool homes on our map.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Little River (8), Loris (5), Socastee area (4) and Aynor (1) each had fewer than 10, too few for a middle price. Across the county line in Georgetown County, 16 of the 117 houses for sale in <a href="/homes/map/?q=house%20with%20a%20pool%20in%20pawleys%20island" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Pawleys Island</a> have a pool.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="cost"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="What they cost">How much does a house with a pool cost in Myrtle Beach?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A private pool comes mostly with the top of the market. Under $500,000, about 1 house in 50 has one. Over $1,000,000, more than half do.</p><style>main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact{padding-top:1rem;padding-bottom:.85rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .cap{margin-bottom:.7rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.1rem .9rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowheader]{line-height:1.3}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .b{height:.5rem;min-width:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .v{line-height:1.25}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact figcaption{margin-top:.8rem}@media (max-width:699px){main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.45rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.05rem}}</style><figure class="c3-gchart c3-gchart-compact"><div role="table" aria-label="Share of houses for sale with their own pool, by asking price"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">Share of houses for sale with their own pool, by asking price</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Asking price</span><span role="columnheader">share with a pool</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row" class="ref"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">$300,000 or less</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.009" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">1%</span></span></div><div role="row" class="ref"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">$300,000 to $400,000</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.02" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">2%</span></span></div><div role="row" class="ref"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">$400,000 to $500,000</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.03" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">3%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">$500,000 to $750,000</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.075" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">8%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">$750,000 to $1,000,000</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.26" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">26%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">$1,000,000 to $1,500,000</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.464" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">46%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">$1,500,000 to $2,000,000</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.596" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">60%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Over $2,000,000</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.698" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">70%</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>Houses for sale in Horry County on November 8, 2026, our count. Under $500,000 the bars are gray.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">At the same size, a house with a pool still asks far more. Houses of 1,500 to 1,999 square feet ask about $515,000 with a pool and $350,000 without. At 3,000 to 3,999 square feet it is about $1,120,000 against $775,000.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">That gap is not the price of a pool. Houses with a pool are more often on the water, about 1 in 5 against 1 in 7, and about 3 in 4 list a fenced yard.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Find a pool home in your price range</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Send us your price and the towns you like. We send back the houses with a pool, and the neighborhoods with a shared pool, that fit.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Send us your price range</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="own-or-shared"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Own pool or shared">Should you buy a house with a pool or use a community pool?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">It comes down to price. A house with its own pool costs more than twice as much in the middle, and a shared pool comes with an HOA fee.</p><ul class="pl-cards" aria-label="Your own pool or a neighborhood pool"><li><span class="k">Your own pool</span><b>About $955,000, the middle asking price</b><p>257 houses for sale list their own pool. About 1 in 4 of them also lists a hot tub. You keep up the fence, the pump and the water.</p><p>A pool at a single-family home, including a short-term rental, is a residential pool. The state pool office (SC DES) does not regulate it. Our <a href="/invest/str-rules/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">short-term rental rules page</a> covers renting the house.</p></li><li><span class="k">A neighborhood pool</span><b>About $435,000, the middle asking price</b><p>About 1,260 houses for sale sit in a neighborhood with a shared pool and have none of their own. Their middle HOA fee is about $105 a month.</p><p>South Carolina counts a subdivision's pool as a public pool. That is the state's category, and it does not mean anyone may swim there. It needs a yearly state permit, and a certified pool operator must check it at least three times a week while it is open.</p></li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A buyer with $500,000 to spend sees the difference at once:</p><div class="pl-stat"><p class="k">On a budget of $500,000 or less</p><ul><li><strong>37</strong><span>houses for sale with their own pool, in all of Horry County</span></li><li><strong>827</strong><span>houses for sale in a neighborhood with a shared pool</span></li></ul></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Our <a href="/buyers/no-hoa-homes/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">homes with no HOA page</a> covers what an HOA can and cannot control, and our <a href="/buyers/gated-communities/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">gated communities page</a> covers neighborhoods with a clubhouse and pool.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="fence"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="The fence rule">Do you need a fence around a pool in Horry County?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes. In Horry County outside city limits, a pool must be enclosed by a fence or other barrier with gates. The county uses the 2021 International Swimming Pool and Spa Code, written by the International Code Council.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">South Carolina has no statewide pool code. Each county or city may adopt one by its own ordinance, so we check which rule covers the house you want.</p><figure class="pl-fig"><p class="cap">Horry County's pool fence rule, outside city limits</p><div class="pl-fig-in"><svg class="pl-svg" viewBox="0 0 480 360" role="img" aria-labelledby="plSvgT" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><title id="plSvgT">Drawing of a backyard pool from above: the house wall on one side and a fence on the other three, a gate that swings out, and the pump outside the fence. Numbers 1 to 6 match the list.</title><rect x="0" y="0" width="480" height="360" rx="18" fill="#fbf8f3"/><rect x="40" y="82" width="360" height="218" fill="#e3ecd6"/><rect x="20" y="16" width="400" height="66" rx="4" fill="#e8dfd2" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-width="2"/><rect x="228" y="70" width="34" height="12" rx="2" fill="#c4783a"/><rect x="112" y="128" width="196" height="116" rx="20" fill="#a9d3e6" stroke="#5c8fa8" stroke-width="3"/><path d="M140 170q12-8 24 0t24 0t24 0M200 205q12-8 24 0t24 0t24 0" fill="none" stroke="#ffffff" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round" opacity=".8"/><path d="M40 82L40 300" stroke="#c4783a" stroke-width="5" stroke-linecap="round"/><path d="M45.0 89.0L35.0 89.0M45.0 103.0L35.0 103.0M45.0 117.0L35.0 117.0M45.0 131.0L35.0 131.0M45.0 145.0L35.0 145.0M45.0 159.0L35.0 159.0M45.0 173.0L35.0 173.0M45.0 187.0L35.0 187.0M45.0 201.0L35.0 201.0M45.0 215.0L35.0 215.0M45.0 229.0L35.0 229.0M45.0 243.0L35.0 243.0M45.0 257.0L35.0 257.0M45.0 271.0L35.0 271.0M45.0 285.0L35.0 285.0M45.0 299.0L35.0 299.0" stroke="#c4783a" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M40 300L190 300" stroke="#c4783a" stroke-width="5" stroke-linecap="round"/><path d="M47.0 295.0L47.0 305.0M61.0 295.0L61.0 305.0M75.0 295.0L75.0 305.0M89.0 295.0L89.0 305.0M103.0 295.0L103.0 305.0M117.0 295.0L117.0 305.0M131.0 295.0L131.0 305.0M145.0 295.0L145.0 305.0M159.0 295.0L159.0 305.0M173.0 295.0L173.0 305.0M187.0 295.0L187.0 305.0" stroke="#c4783a" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M240 300L400 300" stroke="#c4783a" stroke-width="5" stroke-linecap="round"/><path d="M247.0 295.0L247.0 305.0M261.0 295.0L261.0 305.0M275.0 295.0L275.0 305.0M289.0 295.0L289.0 305.0M303.0 295.0L303.0 305.0M317.0 295.0L317.0 305.0M331.0 295.0L331.0 305.0M345.0 295.0L345.0 305.0M359.0 295.0L359.0 305.0M373.0 295.0L373.0 305.0M387.0 295.0L387.0 305.0" stroke="#c4783a" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M400 300L400 82" stroke="#c4783a" stroke-width="5" stroke-linecap="round"/><path d="M395.0 293.0L405.0 293.0M395.0 279.0L405.0 279.0M395.0 265.0L405.0 265.0M395.0 251.0L405.0 251.0M395.0 237.0L405.0 237.0M395.0 223.0L405.0 223.0M395.0 209.0L405.0 209.0M395.0 195.0L405.0 195.0M395.0 181.0L405.0 181.0M395.0 167.0L405.0 167.0M395.0 153.0L405.0 153.0M395.0 139.0L405.0 139.0M395.0 125.0L405.0 125.0M395.0 111.0L405.0 111.0M395.0 97.0L405.0 97.0M395.0 83.0L405.0 83.0" stroke="#c4783a" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M190 300L225.4 335.4" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-width="5" stroke-linecap="round"/><path d="M240 300A50 50 0 0 1 225.4 335.4" fill="none" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/><circle cx="234" cy="293" r="4.5" fill="#1c2028"/><rect x="436" y="168" width="26" height="40" rx="5" fill="#1c2028"/><circle cx="449" cy="182" r="6" fill="none" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M404 222H432M404 216v12M432 216v12" stroke="#8a5226" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round"/><g><circle cx="40" cy="190" r="15" fill="#1c2028"/><text x="40" y="196" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="17" font-weight="700" fill="#fff">1</text></g><g><circle cx="330" cy="300" r="15" fill="#1c2028"/><text x="330" y="306" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="17" font-weight="700" fill="#fff">2</text></g><g><circle cx="258" cy="338" r="15" fill="#1c2028"/><text x="258" y="344" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="17" font-weight="700" fill="#fff">3</text></g><g><circle cx="216" cy="276" r="15" fill="#1c2028"/><text x="216" y="282" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="17" font-weight="700" fill="#fff">4</text></g><g><circle cx="245" cy="48" r="15" fill="#1c2028"/><text x="245" y="54" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="17" font-weight="700" fill="#fff">5</text></g><g><circle cx="449" cy="140" r="15" fill="#1c2028"/><text x="449" y="146" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="17" font-weight="700" fill="#fff">6</text></g></svg><ol class="pl-key" aria-label="What the numbers in the drawing show"><li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">1</span><span><b>Height</b>At least 48 inches tall, measured on the side away from the pool.</span></li><li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">2</span><span><b>Gaps</b>No opening a 4-inch ball could pass through. At most 2 inches under the fence over grass or gravel, or 4 inches over concrete.</span></li><li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">3</span><span><b>The gate</b>It opens outward, away from the pool. It closes by itself and latches by itself.</span></li><li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">4</span><span><b>The latch</b>A latch lower than 54 inches goes on the pool side, at least 3 inches below the top of the gate.</span></li><li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">5</span><span><b>Doors and windows</b>Where the house wall is part of the barrier, each door to the pool and each window with a sill under 48 inches needs an alarm.</span></li><li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">6</span><span><b>The pump</b>Pumps, filters and heaters sit at least 36 inches from the outside of the fence, so no one can use them to climb over it.</span></li></ol></div><figcaption>Our drawing of section 305 of the 2021 International Swimming Pool and Spa Code, as Horry County's pool fence affidavit quotes it.</figcaption></figure><ul class="pl-plain" aria-label="Three more parts of the county's rule"><li><b>Instead of a door alarm</b>The code also allows a listed safety cover on the pool, or another protection the county approves, such as doors that close and latch by themselves.</li><li><b>Covers instead of a fence</b>A hot tub with a lockable safety cover needs no barrier, and neither does a pool with a powered safety cover. Each cover must meet the national safety standard, ASTM F1346.</li><li><b>The permit</b>A new pool needs a county permit and inspections, and may not be used until the county approves them. The permit is good for 180 days, and each inspection renews it for 180 days.</li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The rule is there for small children. Pool and spa drownings reported to the <a href="https://www.cpsc.gov/s3fs-public/Pool-or-Spa-Submersion-Estimated-Nonfatal-Drowning-Injuries-and-Reported-Drownings-2026-Report.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Consumer Product Safety Commission</a> averaged 376 a year for children under 15 in 2021 to 2023. Of those, 77 percent were under 5, and 71 percent of the deaths happened at a home.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="inspection"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="The inspection">Do home inspectors check pools in South Carolina?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Not by default. South Carolina home inspectors work to the <a href="https://www.homeinspector.org/Resources/Standard-of-Practice" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASHI Standard of Practice</a>, and it does not require them to inspect a pool, a hot tub or a fence.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The inspector must say before the inspection what it will not cover, and must mark on the report each item not inspected. The buyer and the inspector can agree to add the pool to the inspection.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Buying a house with a pool?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Tell us which house. We ask the seller for the pool's county permit papers and tell you what the inspection will leave out.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Have us look into the pool</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="costs"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Insurance and tax">What does a pool add to the cost of owning a home here?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A pool can raise the insurance bill, and a pool added after you buy raises the home's value for property tax.</p><ul class="pl-plain" aria-label="What a pool adds to the cost of owning"><li><b>Insurance</b>The <a href="https://content.naic.org/article/consumer_insight_protecting_your_home_coverage_pools_hot_tubs_and_backyard_toys.htm" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">national association of state insurance regulators (NAIC)</a> says insurers may charge more for a pool. They may ask whether it is covered or locked when not in use, and what fence you have. An umbrella policy adds liability coverage above your homeowners policy. Our <a href="/buyers/coastal-insurance/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">coastal insurance page</a> covers the wind and flood policies here.</li><li><b>Property tax</b>In South Carolina, a new improvement such as a pool is added to the home's market value for tax at its full market value. The 15 percent cap on reassessment increases does not apply to it in its first taxed year. When you buy, the county values the home as of December 31 of the year you bought it, and that value is taxed from the next year. Our <a href="/buyers/property-taxes/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">property tax page</a> has the rates.</li><li><b>The swimming season</b>At the North Myrtle Beach airport, the average daily high is above 80 degrees from June through September. It is near 79 in May, 76 in October and about 56 in January. A pool heater is what stretches the season into spring and fall.</li></ul></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="pool-homes-faq"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Common questions</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">Pool homes FAQ</h2><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Are there homes with a pool under $300,000 in Myrtle Beach?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Very few. On November 8, 2026, 5 of the 575 houses for sale in Horry County at $300,000 or less had their own pool. 168 houses at that price sat in a neighborhood with a shared pool.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Does a pool add value to a home in Myrtle Beach?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Houses with a pool ask more at every size, but they also sit nearer the beach and more often on the water. The gap in asking prices is not the value of the pool itself.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Do you need a fence around a pool in South Carolina?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">South Carolina has no statewide pool code; each county or city may adopt one. Horry County requires a barrier at least 48 inches tall around a pool outside city limits.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Do home inspectors check pools in South Carolina?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Not by default. The standard South Carolina home inspectors follow does not require them to inspect a pool, a hot tub or a fence. The buyer and the inspector can agree to add it.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can I rent out a house with a pool in Myrtle Beach?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A pool at a single-family home, including a short-term rental, is a residential pool, and the state pool office does not regulate it. Renting the pool itself out to the public, for example through a pool-sharing app, needs a state public-pool permit.</p><p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:760px;margin-top:1.6rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Sources:</strong> <a href="https://www.horrycountysc.gov/media/r2sebvu1/pool-fence-affidavit-2021-ispsc.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Horry County pool fence affidavit</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/statmast.php?title=6&chapter=9" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 6-9</a>, <a href="https://llr.sc.gov/bcc/PDFfiles/Building%20Codes%20in%20Effect%20for%20South%20Carolina%20Final.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Building Codes Council</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/statmast.php?title=40&chapter=59" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 40-59</a>, <a href="https://www.llr.sc.gov/res/PDF/FAQ/RBI%20FAQ.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC LLR inspector FAQ</a>, <a href="https://www.homeinspector.org/Resources/Standard-of-Practice" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASHI Standard of Practice</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t12c037.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 12-37</a>, <a href="https://www.des.sc.gov/programs/bureau-water/recreational-waters/rental-residential-swimming-pools-public" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC DES rental pools</a>, <a href="https://www.des.sc.gov/sites/des/files/Library/Regulations/R.61-51.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC DES R.61-51</a>, <a href="https://content.naic.org/article/consumer_insight_protecting_your_home_coverage_pools_hot_tubs_and_backyard_toys.htm" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NAIC</a>, <a href="https://www.cpsc.gov/s3fs-public/Pool-or-Spa-Submersion-Estimated-Nonfatal-Drowning-Injuries-and-Reported-Drownings-2026-Report.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CPSC 2026 report</a>, <a href="https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/products/land-based-station/us-climate-normals" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NOAA climate normals</a>. Houses for sale: our count of the listings on our site, Horry County, November 8, 2026, in aggregate, asking prices only. Source: Coastal Carolinas Association of REALTORS® Multiple Listing Service (CCMLS). Weather: NOAA 1991-2020 normals, North Myrtle Beach airport.</p><p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:760px;margin-top:1rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Legal notice.</strong> Chapter3 Realty Corp is a licensed South Carolina real estate brokerage. We are not attorneys, insurers or tax advisers, and nothing on this page is legal, insurance or tax advice. For the rule on one property, ask the county or city building office or a South Carolina attorney.</p><div class="c3ph-credits"><div class="c3ph-h" id="photo-credits">Photo credits</div><div role="list" aria-labelledby="photo-credits"><div role="listitem">Four beach houses in a row, each with a small fenced pool between the house and the dunes, with wooden walkways over the dunes to the beach. Photo: U.S. Geological Survey, <a href="https://www.usa.gov/publicdomain/label/1.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Public domain</a>, via <a href="https://doi.org/10.5066/F7N877XX" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U.S. Geological Survey</a>.</div></div></div><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@graph":[{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://chapter3realty.com/images/2026-11-n/buyers-pool-homes/surfside-beach-houses-with-pools-2011-1200w.webp","url":"https://chapter3realty.com/images/2026-11-n/buyers-pool-homes/surfside-beach-houses-with-pools-2011-1200w.webp","width":1200,"height":675,"encodingFormat":"image/webp","name":"Four beach houses in a row, each with a small fenced pool between the house and the dunes, with wooden walkways over the dunes to the beach","caption":"Four oceanfront houses in Surfside Beach, each with its own pool behind the dunes, seen from a plane in 2011.","description":"Four beach houses in a row, each with a small fenced pool between the house and the dunes, with wooden walkways over the dunes to the beach","creator":{"@type":"Organization","name":"U.S. Geological Survey"},"creditText":"U.S","copyrightNotice":"Public domain (U.S. Geological Survey)","license":"https://www.usa.gov/publicdomain/label/1.0/","acquireLicensePage":"https://doi.org/10.5066/F7N877XX","isBasedOn":"https://doi.org/10.5066/F7N877XX","contentLocation":{"@type":"Place","name":"The oceanfront of the Town of Surfside Beach (Horry County tax district 660), Surfside Beach, South Carolina"},"representativeOfPage":true}]}</script></div></section><section style="background:var(--navy);padding:3rem 0"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:cente