ity inspections. Deliver consistent, trusted results that set you apart from the competition. 1. Introduction div[data-counter="0"] ol { counter-reset: i !important; } div[data-counter="0"] ol li::before { counter-increment: i; content: "1." counter(i) " " !important; } div[data-counter="0"] ol li li::before { content: counter(i, upper-alpha) ". " !important; left: -2rem; } div[data-counter="0"] ol li li li::before { content: counter(i) ". " !important; } div[data-counter="0"] ol li li li li::before { content: counter(i, lower-alpha) ". " !important; } #site-content div[data-counter="0"] ol li li::before { left: -2rem; } #site-content div[data-counter="0"] div>ol>li { padding-left: 3rem; } #site-content div[data-counter="0"] div>ol>li { padding-left: 3rem; } #site-content div[data-counter="0"] div>ol>li::before { left: 0; } #site-content div[data-counter="0"] div>ol li li li::before { left: -2rem; } #site-content div[data-counter="0"] div>ol li li li:nth-child(n+9)::before { left: -3rem; } The American Society of Home Inspectors®, Inc. (ASHI®) is a not-for-profit professional society established in 1976. Membership in ASHI is voluntary, and its members are private home inspectors . ASHI’s objectives include promoting excellence within the profession and the continual improvement of its members’ inspection services to the public. 2. PURPOSE AND SCOPE div[data-counter="1"] ol { counter-reset: i !important; } div[data-counter="1"] ol li::before { counter-increment: i; content: "2." counter(i) " " !important; } div[data-counter="1"] ol li li::before { content: counter(i, upper-alpha) ". " !important; left: -2rem; } div[data-counter="1"] ol li li li::before { content: counter(i) ". " !important; } div[data-counter="1"] ol li li li li::before { content: counter(i, lower-alpha) ". " !important; } #site-content div[data-counter="1"] ol li li::before { left: -2rem; } #site-content div[data-counter="1"] div>ol>li { padding-left: 3rem; } #site-content div[data-counter="1"] div>ol>li { padding-left: 3rem; } #site-content div[data-counter="1"] div>ol>li::before { left: 0; } #site-content div[data-counter="1"] div>ol li li li::before { left: -2rem; } #site-content div[data-counter="1"] div>ol li li li:nth-child(n+9)::before { left: -3rem; } The purpose of this document is to establish a minimum standard (Standard) for home inspections performed by home inspectors who subscribe to this Standard. Home inspections performed using this Standard are intended to provide the client with information about the condition of inspected systems and components at the time of the home inspection . The inspector shall: Inspect readily accessible , visually observable, installed systems and components listed in this Standard. Provide the client with a written report, using a format and medium selected by the inspector that states: those systems and components inspected that, in the professional judgment of the inspector, are not functioning properly, significantly deficient, unsafe , or are near the end of their service lives, recommendations to correct, or monitor for future correction, the deficiencies reported in 2.2.B.1, or items needing further evaluation (Per Exclusion 13.2.A.5 the inspector is NOT required to determine methods, materials, or costs of corrections.), reasoning or explanation as to the nature of the deficiencies reported in 2.2.B.1, that are not self-evident, those systems and components designated for inspection in this Standard that were present at the time of the home inspection but were not inspected and the reason(s) why. adhere to the ASHI® Code of Ethics for the Home Inspection Profession. This Standard is not intended to limit the inspector from: including other services or systems and components in addition to those required in Section 2.2.A. designing or specifying repairs, provided the inspector is appropriately qualified and willing to do so. excluding systems and components from the inspection if requested or agreed to by the client. 3. STRUCTURAL COMPONENTS div[data-counter="2"] ol { counter-reset: i !important; } div[data-counter="2"] ol li::before { counter-increment: i; content: "3." counter(i) " " !important; } div[data-counter="2"] ol li li::before { content: counter(i, upper-alpha) ". " !important; left: -2rem; } div[data-counter="2"] ol li li li::before { content: counter(i) ". " !important; } div[data-counter="2"] ol li li li li::before { content: counter(i, lower-alpha) ". " !important; } #site-content div[data-counter="2"] ol li li::before { left: -2rem; } #site-content div[data-counter="2"] div>ol>li { padding-left: 3rem; } #site-content div[data-counter="2"] div>ol>li { padding-left: 3rem; } #site-content div[data-counter="2"] div>ol>li::before { left: 0; } #site-content div[data-counter="2"] div>ol li li li::before { left: -2rem; } #site-content div[data-counter="2"] div>ol li li li:nth-child(n+9)::before { left: -3rem; } The inspector shall: inspect structural components, including the foundation and framing. describe : the methods used to inspect under-floor crawlspaces and attics. the foundation. the floor structure. the wall structure. the ceiling structure. the roof structure. The inspector is NOT required to: Provide engineering or architectural services or analysis. Offer an opinion about the adequacy of structural systems and components. Enter under-floor crawlspace areas with less than 24 inches of vertical clearance between components and the ground or have an access opening smaller than 16 inches by 24 inches. Traverse attic load-bearing components that are concealed by insulation or by other materials. 4. EXTERIOR div[data-counter="3"] ol { counter-reset: i !important; } div[data-counter="3"] ol li::before { counter-increment: i; content: "4." counter(i) " " !important; } div[data-counter="3"] ol li li::before { content: counter(i, upper-alpha) ". " !important; left: -2rem; } div[data-counter="3"] ol li li li::before { content: counter(i) ". " !important; } div[data-counter="3"] ol li li li li::before { content: counter(i, lower-alpha) ". " !important; } #site-content div[data-counter="3"] ol li li::before { left: -2rem; } #site-content div[data-counter="3"] div>ol>li { padding-left: 3rem; } #site-content div[data-counter="3"] div>ol>li { padding-left: 3rem; } #site-content div[data-counter="3"] div>ol>li::before { left: 0; } #site-content div[data-counter="3"] div>ol li li li::before { left: -2rem; } #site-content div[data-counter="3"] div>ol li li li:nth-child(n+9)::before { left: -3rem; } The inspector shall: Inspect : Wall coverings , flashing, and trim. Exterior doors. Attached and adjacent decks, balconies, stoops, steps, porches, and their associated railings. Eaves, soffits, and fascias accessible from the ground level. Vegetation, grading, surface drainage, and retaining walls that are likely to to affect the building adversely. Adjacent and entryway walkways, patios, and driveways. Describe wall coverings . The inspector is NOT required to inspect : Screening, shutters, awnings, and similar seasonal accessories. Fences, boundary walls, and similar structures. Geological and soil conditions. Recreational facilities . Outbuildings other than garages and carports. Seawalls, break-walls, and docks. Erosion control and earth stabilization measures. 5. ROOFING div[data-counter="4"] ol { counter-reset: i !important; } div[data-counter="4"] ol li::before { counter-increment: i; content: "5." counter(i) " " !important; } div[data-counter="4"] ol li li::before { content: counter(i, upper-alpha) ". " !important; left: -2rem; } div[data-counter="4"] ol li li li::before { content: counter(i) ". " !important; } div[data-counter="4"] ol li li li li::before { content: counter(i, lower-alpha) ". " !important; } #site-content div[data-counter="4"] ol li li::before { left: -2rem; } #site-content div[data-counter="4"] div>ol>li { padding-left: 3rem; } #site-content div[data-counter="4"] div>ol>li { padding-left: 3rem; } #site-con